        {
            "number": 8,
            "revision": "103e75dc681e25f0a441b649a10f612cfe32269c",
            "parents": [
                "06f7bf712481106f8131ec7986381e521dc3dd77"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/17/79117/8",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Potin Lai",
                "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                "username": "potinlai"
            },
            "createdOn": 1746031677,
            "author": {
                "name": "Potin Lai",
                "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                "username": "potinlai"
            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Can we get a lot more information in the commit message as to the system topology that is driving us to go this direction?  I know there is a mux in the path, but I thought MCTP/PLDM generally could work over a mux but just not for firmware upgrade."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "Add more information in commit message."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Is there some way we can manipulate the muxes without subverting the kernel control here?  Having to do `i2ctransfer` calls in a shell script is really not ideal."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "Sorry, I don't have better idea for now.\n\nWe need to keep the I2C mux connected to ensure that device responses are not blocked during PLDM communication. However, to avoid bus address conflict, the mux must be disconnected when another mux on the same bus is actively accessing its downstream devices.\n\nNot sure this behavior need to be handleed at kernel driver or userspace (pldmd)?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 80,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm confused by this.  If we can get the temperature via PLDM why aren't we using the PLDM daemon for it?  That has sensor-monitoring built right in."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Hopefully this isn't necessary?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "Removed."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 159,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Do we need to do something here to set the external sensor to offline?  What happens if we booted and then powered off?  Are we going to be stuck with stale sensor values forever?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 159,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "Add set_all_external_sensors_invalid() to set value of all external sensors to nan."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 164,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why does a pldm sensor error result in us assuming the host is off?  Is this just a nice way to clean up the MCTP stack?  Maybe `host_off_cleanup` is a poor name?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-catalina/plat-svc/files/iob-nic-temp-read",
                    "line": 164,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "Due to pldmtool might stuck few senconds when NIC not responding (host off), I add host state check in poll_numeric_sensor(), and return 1 if host is off.\n\nThis is for skipping rest of sensor polling to avoid pldmtool get block multiple times."
                }
            ],
